---
/**
 * Terms of use.
 *
 * Deliberately short, because the site is small: static informational pages with no account, no
 * transaction and no user-submitted content. A long agreement describing subscriptions, user
 * accounts and submitted data would be describing a product that does not exist.
 *
 * The independence and no-legal-advice statements are not boilerplate — they are the same
 * commitments AGENTS.md sections 1.3 and 1.4 bind the product to, and they are the ones that matter
 * for a tool people consult while deciding what to do about a disrupted flight.
 *
 * docs/agents/ROSTER.md section 3 assigns the policy pages to trust-compliance-officer; this is a
 * pre-Phase-11 baseline added under direct owner instruction because the site had no terms page.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const title = 'Terms — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'Terms of use for DelayPilot: informational only, independent of any airline or regulator, and not legal advice.'

const lastUpdated = '29 August 2026'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} nav="terms">
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Terms of use</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Short, because the site is small. It will grow when there is something more to govern.
    </p>
    <p class="meta">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What this site is</h2>

      <p>
        DelayPilot is an independent travel-information tool. As of the date above it consists of
        static informational pages. There is no account, no purchase, no subscription, and no way to
        submit content.
      </p>

      <h2>Independence</h2>

      <p>
        DelayPilot is <strong>not</strong> an airline, airport, government agency, regulator, law firm,
        claims-management company, or flight-data provider, and is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or
        acting on behalf of any of them. Airline and airport names or codes, where they appear, are used
        only to identify a flight or location.
      </p>

      <h2>Not legal advice</h2>

      <p>
        Passenger-rights information on this site is general and informational. It describes what
        published rules <strong>may</strong> mean for a described situation, tied to a dated, versioned
        rule set with links to the official source. It is not legal advice, not a determination of your
        rights, and not a statement that any airline owes you anything. Whether a rule applies to your
        specific case depends on facts this site does not have.
      </p>

      <p>
        For a decision with money or a missed connection riding on it, check the airline's own terms
        and the relevant regulator, both of which are linked wherever this site cites them.
      </p>

      <h2>Accuracy and availability</h2>

      <p>
        Operational flight information, when it ships, comes from third-party providers and can be
        incomplete, delayed, or wrong. Where the site cannot source a value it says so rather than
        estimating one. The site is provided as-is, without warranty, and may be unavailable or
        changed at any time.
      </p>

      <p>
        DelayPilot never files, submits, or purchases anything on your behalf, and never
        authenticates as you to a third party.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>

      <p>
        These terms will be updated as the product gains features that need governing. The date at
        the top records when they last matched the site.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
